## Previous step outcome
step 5: Attempted: replace the scalar seed with a wmma tensor-core kernel (m16n16k16 bf16, BLOCK_M=64, BLOCK_N=64, 4 warps, exp2 online softmax, GQA support).

Outcome: it compiles and is numerically correct — max_abs_err 0.00183 vs threshold 0.0255 (S=1024) and 0.00089 vs 0.00928 (S=4096) — but measured throughput is only ~2.36–2.40 TFLOPS. It was not a committed improvement, so treat this as a performance regression, not a compile/correctness failure.

Reuse this verified layout:

> Fragment layouts (verified by probe on sm_86): for m16n16k16, the C accumulator layout equals the matrix_a layout: lane l (gid=l/4, t=l%4) holds elements  
> x0=(gid,2t) x1=(gid,2t+1) x2=(gid+8,2t) x3=(gid+8,2t+1)  
> x4=(gid,2t+8) x5=(gid,2t+9) x6=(gid+8,2t+8) x7=(gid+8,2t+9)  
> so the softmaxed S accumulator converts to the P matrix_a fragment in place (no shuffles). K tiles are consumed as matrix_b col_major (ldm 128), V tiles as matrix_b row_major (ldm 128).

Also keep the exp2-domain softmax (`exp2_approx` via `ex2.approx.f32`) and the GQA base index formula.

Why it was slow: K/V staging is single-buffered synchronous `uint4` loads plus a `__syncthreads()` every tile; no `cp.async`, no double buffering

